{"id":443,"date":"2018-11-22T14:49:25","date_gmt":"2018-11-22T14:49:25","guid":{"rendered":"https:\/\/www.domainconnect.org\/?p=443"},"modified":"2019-03-25T15:39:33","modified_gmt":"2019-03-25T15:39:33","slug":"spec-updates-dectecting-txt-record-conflicts","status":"publish","type":"post","link":"https:\/\/www.domainconnect.org\/spec-updates-dectecting-txt-record-conflicts\/","title":{"rendered":"Spec Updates: Dectecting TXT Record Conflicts"},"content":{"rendered":"<p>We tempted fate at ICANN. A few of us were chatting about how we haven&#8217;t had a spec update in months.<\/p>\n<p>Naturally we all came home and something came up.<\/p>\n<p>We&#8217;ve always known that detecting conflicts in TXT records was a bit optimistic. The specification had indicated that adding a TXT record should not detect conflicts with existing TXT records. But we got some feedback that this behavior wasn&#8217;t always desired.<\/p>\n<p>As such, we&#8217;ve added some simple rules around how conflicts in TXT records should be seen. There are three possible settings for a new value called &#8220;txtConflictMatchingMode&#8221; as an attribute of a TXT record in a template.<\/p>\n<p>None: This is the default value, and is the existing behavior. With this setting, a template would behave as it always has.<\/p>\n<p>All: This value indicates that existing values should be detected as conflicts.<\/p>\n<p>Prefix: This value indicates that existing values with the same prefix (specified in another attribute txtConfictMatchingPrefix).<\/p>\n<p>For detail section 6.2 of the specification.<\/p>\n","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"<p>We tempted fate at ICANN. A few of us were chatting about how we haven&#8217;t had a spec update in&hellip;<\/p>\n","protected":false},"author":1,"featured_media":0,"comment_status":"closed","ping_status":"open","sticky":false,"template":"","format":"standard","meta":{"footnotes":""},"categories":[5],"tags":[],"class_list":["post-443","post","type-post","status-publish","format-standard","hentry","category-specification"],"_links":{"self":[{"href":"https:\/\/www.domainconnect.org\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/posts\/443","targetHints":{"allow":["GET"]}}],"collection":[{"href":"https:\/\/www.domainconnect.org\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/posts"}],"about":[{"href":"https:\/\/www.domainconnect.org\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/types\/post"}],"author":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/www.domainconnect.org\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/users\/1"}],"replies":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/www.domainconnect.org\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/comments?post=443"}],"version-history":[{"count":1,"href":"https:\/\/www.domainconnect.org\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/posts\/443\/revisions"}],"predecessor-version":[{"id":444,"href":"https:\/\/www.domainconnect.org\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/posts\/443\/revisions\/444"}],"wp:attachment":[{"href":"https:\/\/www.domainconnect.org\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media?parent=443"}],"wp:term":[{"taxonomy":"category","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/www.domainconnect.org\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/categories?post=443"},{"taxonomy":"post_tag","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/www.domainconnect.org\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/tags?post=443"}],"curies":[{"name":"wp","href":"https:\/\/api.w.org\/{rel}","templated":true}]}}